    "description": "Ascella QPU",
    "detector": "threshold",
    "documentation": "<p><strong>qpu:ascella</strong></p>\n\n<p>QPU Ascella is one of our online quantum computers. It features 12 available modes and up to 6 photons. With Ascella, you can implement any unitary transform on 12 modes -- it is universal. A schematic of the chip is shown above.</p>\n\n<p>The detectors are not number-resolving (i.e. cannot differentiate between 1- and n-photon states).</p>\n\n<p>Depending on your algorithm, you may be interested in the n-photon coincidence rate. You can calculate it as Clock [MHz] x (Transmittance)^n.</p>\n\n<p>The QPU supports sampling commands: samples and sample_count.</p>\n\n<p>For more information about the Quandela Cloud and how to get started, refer to the <a style=\"color: #0052cc;\n      padding: 3px;\n      background-color: rgba(123, 163, 215, 0.3);\n      border-radius: 3px;\" href=\"https://cloud.quandela.com/webide/documentation\" target=\"_blank\">Documentation</a>. Information about using Perceval to interact with our cloud computers is available <a style=\"color: #0052cc;\n      padding: 3px;\n      background-color: rgba(123, 163, 215, 0.3);\n      border-radius: 3px;\" href=\"https://perceval.quandela.net/docs/index.html\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>. And if you have any questions, send us a message by clicking the Support button in the bottom right of any Cloud page.</p>\n",
