    "description": "Belenos QPU",
    "detector": "threshold",
    "documentation": "<p><strong>qpu:belenos</strong></p>\n\n<p>QPU Belenos is our online quantum computer. It features 24 available modes and up to 12 photons. Belenos chip architecture is universal, meaning any unitary transform can be implemented. A schematic of the chip is shown above.</p>\n\n<p>Some of the detectors are quasi number-resolving (i.e. they can differentiate between 1- and n-photon states).</p>\n\n<p>Depending on your algorithm, you may be interested in the n-photon coincidence rate. You can calculate it as Clock [MHz] x (Transmittance x Brightness)^n.</p>\n\n<p>For more information about the Quandela Cloud and how to get started, refer to the <a style=\"color: #0052cc;\n      padding: 3px;\n      background-color: rgba(123, 163, 215, 0.3);\n      border-radius: 3px;\" href=\"https://cloud.quandela.com/webide/documentation\" target=\"_blank\">Documentation</a>. Information about using Perceval to interact with our cloud computers is available <a style=\"color: #0052cc;\n      padding: 3px;\n      background-color: rgba(123, 163, 215, 0.3);\n      border-radius: 3px;\" href=\"https://perceval.quandela.net/docs/index.html\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>. And if you have any questions, send us a message by clicking the Support button in the bottom right of any Cloud page.</p>\n",
